              %em Zwei äquivalente Vielheiten sind entweder beide ‘Mengen’ oder beide inkonsistent.
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Georg+Cantor"} Georg Cantor
              (1899)
              %sup#fnref:1
                %a{:href => "#fn:1", :rel => "footnote"} 1
          %p
            %strong Axioms of collection
            and replacement are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+schemata"} axiom schemata
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
            that permit to construct new
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
            from other already given sets, thereby contributing substantially to the size of the set-theoretic
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universe"} universe
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundational+axioms"} foundational axioms
          %p
            The most famous of these
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+schemata"} axiom schemata
            is the
            %strong axiom of replacement
            %sup#fnref:2
              %a{:href => "#fn:2", :rel => "footnote"} 2
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Zermelo-Fraenkel+set+theory"} Zermelo-Fraenkel set theory
            that was suggested by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Abraham+Fraenkel"} A. Fraenkel
            and formulated by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thoralf+Skolem"} T. Skolem
            in 1922. Given a unary operation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            and a set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            it permits to collect all
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(y)
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y\in x
            into a new set.
          %p
            The resulting expansiveness of the set-theoretic universe is somewhat peripheral to the practice of ‘ordinary’ mathematics and therefore a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structural+set+theory"} structural set theory
            like
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"} ETCS
            can omit replacement without incurring a great loss
            %sup#fnref:3>
              %a{:href => "#fn:3", :rel => "footnote"} 3
            \. Even in the context of a ZF-equivalent material set theory the axiom of replacement can be traded in for a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflection+principle"} reflection principle
            = succeed "." do
              %sup#fnref:4
                %a{:href => "#fn:4", :rel => "footnote"} 4
          %p
            Axioms of replacement and collection become useful, however, whenever
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/recursion"} recursively
            constructing a set that is ‘larger’ than any set known before:
          %blockquote
            %p
              what the axiom of replacement is mainly needed for in mathematical practice is to define families of sets indexed by some set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
              carrying some inductive structure as, typically, the set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
              of natural numbers.
              %sup#fnref:5
                %a{:href => "#fn:5", :rel => "footnote"} 5
          %p There are many variations on these axiom schemata, but any given system should only need one.
          %h2#statements Statements
          %p
            In general, these axioms apply to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/binary+relation"} binary relation
            that relates elements of one
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            to arbitrary sets. However, we do
            %em not
            expect that the relation itself be an object in the theory; really, we have an axiom schema with one axiom for every binary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicate"} predicate
            of the proper form. We will write this predicate as
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi(x,Y)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            stands for an elment of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            stands for any set. (Note that there may well be other free variables in the predicate.)
          %p
            Generally,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            will need to be an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/entire+relation"} entire relation
            for the axiom to apply; that is, the axiom has as a hypothesis that, for every
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi(x,Y)
            holds. In versions called ‘replacement’ instead of ‘collection’,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            also needs to be
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functional+relation"} functional
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in A
            %em unique
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi(x,Y)
            holds. Thus, most of the ‘replacement’ versions only make sense if the language has a notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equality"} equality
            of sets.
          %p
            So much for the hypothesis of the axiom; the conclusion asserts the existence of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/family+of+sets"} family of sets
            to which appropriate
            = succeed "s" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℱ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{F}
            such that certain
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℱ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y \in \mathcal{F}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi E
                  %mo →
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\colon E \to I
            such that each
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibre"} fibre
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^*(x)
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in I
            is equal to (or at least isomorphic to) certain
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            to be
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
          .query
            %p Who wants to write out some of these?
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %strong Bounded replacement
                or
                %strong restricted replacement
                or
                = succeed ":" do
                  %strong
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi Δ
                            %mn 0
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta_0
                    \-replacement
                = succeed "-formula" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi Δ
                          %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta_0
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo ,
                        %mi y
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi(x, y)
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi a
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in a
                there exists a unique
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
                with
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo ,
                        %mi y
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi(x, y)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mo ∃
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi a
                      %mo .
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{y \vert \exists x \in a.\phi(x, y)\}
                exists.
            %li
              %p
                %strong> Full replacement
                \: for any formula
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo ,
                        %mi y
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi(x, y)
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi a
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi a
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in a
                there exists a unique
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
                with
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo ,
                        %mi y
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi(x, y)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mo ∃
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi a
                      %mo .
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{y \vert \exists x \in a.\phi(x, y)\}
                exists.
          %h2#lawvere_on_replacement Lawvere on replacement
          %blockquote
            %p
              A question that has been much of a “foundational” interest, though of hardly any significance for the practice of algebra, topology, functional analysis, etc. is whether, for a given
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              can be represented by
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                    %mo →
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E\to T
              for some
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E
              and some mapping; if “imaginable” is interpreted to mean “definable”, an affirmative answer to this question is essentially equivalent (for abstract, constant sets) to the postulation of the so-called “replacement schema”, whereas if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒮
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{S}
              is considered as an object in some larger realm, an affirmative answer means that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒮
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{S}
              itself has “inaccessible cardinality”. However, in view of practice and in view of the role of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒮
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{S}
              as a limiting case of the general notion of continuously variable sets, it seems appropriate to simply define “an internal-to-
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒮
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{S}
              = succeed "-parametrized" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              = succeed "\u201D" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒮
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{S}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒮
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{S}
              with domain
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi T
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              %a{:href => "#Lawvere76"} Lawvere (1976, p.121)
          %p
            See also the remarks on pages 721 and 727 of (
            %a{:href => "#Lawvere00"}> Lawvere 2000
            ).
          %h2#related_discussion Related discussion
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thomas+Streicher"}> Thomas Streicher
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"} William Lawvere
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Colin+McLarty"} Colin McLarty
                et al,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Categorical formulations of replacement
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.mta.ca/~cat-dist/archive/2008/08-3"} link
            %li
              %p
                MO-discussion:
                %em Who needs Replacement anyway ?
                (
                %a{:href => "http://mathoverflow.net/questions/208711/who-needs-replacement-anyway"}> link
                )
            %li
              %p
                Akihiro Kanamori (2012).
                = succeed "." do
                  %em In Praise of Replacement
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "http://math.bu.edu/people/aki/20.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                \-category Cafe (2021),
                %a{:href => "https://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2021/07/large_sets_12.html"} Large Sets 12
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tom+Leinster"}> Tom Leinster
                ) and
                %a{:href => "https://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2021/07/large_sets_125.html"} Large Sets 12.5
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mike+Shulman"}> Mike Shulman
                ) – includes discussion of a number of different replacement axioms for
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"} ETCS
                and more general
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structural+set+theory"} structural set theory
          %h2#related_entries Related entries
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Zermelo-Fraenkel+set+theory"} Zermelo-Fraenkel set theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflection+principle"} reflection principle
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"}> ETCS
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SEAR"} SEAR
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/large+cardinals"} large cardinals
